4. The defendant Nos. 1, 3 and 4 are the vessel &#39;MV Chosun Hope&#39;, he owner and person interested in the vessel &#39;MV Chosun Hope&#39; and the local agent of the vessel respectively. The vessel &#39;MV Chosun Hope&#39; was arrested on the prayer of the plaintiff and upon furnishing Bank Guarantee for Taka 32,00,000 the same was released from the arrest. The Ocean Wave Shipping Ltd entered appearance in this suit and contested the same by filing written statement on 10-3-1999. It has been asserted in the written statement that the suit is not maintainable in this Admiralty Court; that the plaintiff has no locus standi to file this suit and the suit is bad for misjoinder and non-joinder of parties and on the principle of estoppels, waiver and acquiescence and also denied all the material allegations made in the plaint. It has been asserted further by the defendants that the vessel &#39;MV Chosun Hope&#39; loaded the cargo of the plaintiff at the Lian Youngang Port, China for discharging the same at Chittagong, Bangladesh and accordingly, the said vessel sailed from the loading port from China on 29-5-1997 and arrived at Chittagong on 20-6-1997 and there was no delay on the arrival of the vessel. It has been denied that the owners had any contract will the plaintiff that if there has been any delay in arrival of the vessel, the plaintiff should seek remedy from the supplier, as there cannot be any specific time within which the ocean going vessel will arrive at the discharge port since the vessel has to pass an ocean from where the same may face enormous hazard including act of god/peril of sea/accident or mechanical trouble. In the instant case the statement of facts of the deck log book will reveal that there was no unreasonable delay or any delay for the vessel to arrive by 26-6-1997. The date alleged by the plaintiff in the charter party cannot be binding date as alleged by the plaintiff as has been provided in the charter party and accordingly the allegations, against the defendants for late arrival of the vessel is not tenable in the eye of law and facts. The owner of the vessel never undertook to discharge the cargo with the consignee within the fixed specific time. The vessel sailed from the port of China with the cargo of the plaintiff in the same condition as was loaded and arrived at Chittagong Port on 26-6-1997 and it plaintiff was owning to the negligees of the plaintiff that the plaintiff failed to submits that Bill of Entry before the Customs authority within 30-6-1997 against the imported cargo and due to delay in submitting the Bill of Entry before 30-6-1997, the plaintiff might have been effected by the development surcharge on the imported cargo which has been effected from the date of tabling of the Finance Bill in the Parliament for which the defendants cannot be liable. The plaintiff had to pay the development surcharge of 2.5% and the same would be recoverable by the plaintiff through marketing of the goods by inclusion of the same with the sale price. The plaintiff also filed two Writ Petitions challenging the imposition of the development surcharge upon his cargo and he did not pay the surcharge. The vessel in the meantime completed discharge of the cargo and as per final discharge report only 8 MT i.e. 212 Bags of cargo were found to be short landed which has been certified by the Master of the ship and such shortage cannot be treated as short landing of the cargo as against the huge quantity of bangs carried by the vessel and therefore, the claim of the plaintiff on this ground is not tenable in the eye of law. The plaintiff illegally obtained the order of arrest of the vessel and the owner of the vessel has suffered irreparable financial loss and damage and, as such, the plaintiff reserves the right to claim for the losses and damage from the defendant. Although the vessel has completed the discharge of the cargo on 25-7-1997 it was detained due to illegal arrest order. The suit being not maintainable and liable to be dismissed, the Bank Guarantee submitted by the defendant, being No. LG /1501 /1997/1264 dated 7-9-1997 for an amount of Taka 32,00,000 issued by ANZ Grindlays Bank, Dhaka is liable to be returned to the defendants.&nbsp; 

 (4) Is the suit bad for misjoinder and non-joinder of the parties? 

 27. It appears that from the addendum No. 1 that the vessel &#39;MV Chosun Hope&#39; is younger in age and although none of the parties have adduced any evidence as to the maximum speed of the vessel &#39;MV Chosun Hope&#39;, yet from the DW weight of the vessel and other attending facts and circumstances of the case and also from the materials on record it appears that such a younger ocean going vessel can proceed with a maximum speed of 9-12 nautical miles per hour. Exhibit &#39;A&#39; is the lay time statement of facts and the Exhibit &#39;B&#39; is the copy of deck log book of the vessel &#39;MV Chosun Hope&#39; filed by the defendants which being uncomforted, shows that the vessel commenced its journey from port Liang Youngang, China, on 29-5-1997 at 21-30 hours which stopped on the high sea on 1-6-1997 at night 00-45 hours for the purpose of its repairing to the engine and later resumed its journey on 16-46 hours on 2-6-1997. Thereafter, it anchored at Hongkong Port for an inspection on 6-6-1997 at 02-00 hours wherefrom it resumed journey on the same date at 15-00 hours and reached Singapore Port on 14-6-1997 at 07-10 hours, where it waited for Charterer&#39;s instruction upon 19-45 hours of 19-6-1996. Thereafter it resumed its journey to reach outer anchorage of Chittagong Port on 26-6-1997 at 13-32 hours.&nbsp; 

 39. It appears that Exhibit 9 has not been admitted by the defendants which was drafted on a Bangladesh yellow cartridge paper having embed mark XXIV and nowhere within the four corner of the said agreement it has been mentioned as to the place of its execution which is a vital aspect as to its genuinely as one of the executants is a foreigner. Moreover, none of the attesting witnesses of the said agreement Bijoy Chowdhury or Anisuddin Mahmood have been examined before this Court for the purpose of proving the gentility of execution of Exhibit No. 9. As the defendants categorically denied the execution of this document the onus to prove the same lies on this holder of the plaintiff under the Provision of Section 103 of the Evidence Act, 1872 who did not prove the document while examined as PW 1. Therefore the Exhibit No. 9 is not entitled to be considered by this Court for the purpose of admission by the defendant as to the claim to the plaintiff. Therefore, the argument on this Exhibit No. 9 as advance by the learned Senior Advocate Dr M Zahir falls flat. Accordingly, the issue No. 8 is disposed of in favour of the defendant and against the plaintiff.&nbsp;
